//? Title: Estimating treatment effects from observational data
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Simulate a study where a baseline covariate influences both treatment and
outcome. Use an assumed DAG to select an adjustment covariate, then compare
g-computation, inverse probability weighting and augmented weighting.
)

> Read the adjustment problem from the DAG

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/backdoor_dag.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const datamunge::causal::DAG graph(3, {{0, 1}, {0, 2}, {1, 2}});
    graph.diagram({{5, 3}, {0, 0}, {10, 0}}, {"Baseline", "Treatment", "Outcome"})
        .save_svg("build/backdoor_dag.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/backdoor_dag.svg)
// result_end

> Identify an adjustment set and estimate the effect

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/treatment_effects.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace dm = datamunge;

int main() {
    const dm::causal::DAG graph(3, {{0, 1}, {0, 2}, {1, 2}});
    const auto adjustment = graph.backdoor_adjustment(1, 2).value();
    constexpr std::size_t n = 500;
    dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> covariates(n, adjustment.size());
    std::vector<int> treatment(n);
    std::vector<double> outcome(n);
    std::mt19937_64 rng(128);
    std::normal_distribution<double> normal;
    std::uniform_real_distribution<double> uniform;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        const double baseline = normal(rng);
        covariates(i, 0) = baseline;
        treatment[i] = uniform(rng) < 1 / (1 + std::exp(-baseline));
        outcome[i] = 1 + 2 * treatment[i] + 1.5 * baseline + normal(rng);
    }

    const auto fit = dm::causal::fit_binary_ate(outcome, treatment, covariates);
    std::cout << "Adjustment node " << adjustment[0] << ". Estimated propensity range "
              << fit.min_propensity << " to " << fit.max_propensity << ".\n\n"
              << "Raw " << fit.difference_in_means << ", g-computation " << fit.g_computation
              << ", IPW " << fit.ipw << ", AIPW " << fit.aipw << ".\n";
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
        .bars({1, 2, 3, 4}, {fit.difference_in_means, fit.g_computation, fit.ipw, fit.aipw})
        .abline_h(2).title("Estimated treatment effects").y_label("Mean outcome difference")
        .x_ticks({1, 2, 3, 4}, {"Raw", "G-computation", "IPW", "AIPW"})
        .save_svg("build/treatment_effects.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Adjustment node 0. Estimated propensity range 0.0443045 to 0.960241.

Raw 3.35464, g-computation 2.09618, IPW 2.07535, AIPW 2.03982.
\image(figures/treatment_effects.svg)
// result_end

The horizontal line marks the generating effect of 2. G-computation averages
outcome predictions for both treatment states over the same covariates. IPW
balances the groups by their fitted treatment probabilities and AIPW combines
a propensity model with the outcome predictions. These are point estimates
under consistency, positivity and no unmeasured confounding. Extreme fitted
propensities are a warning that some comparisons have little support.
